WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:04.692 --> 00:00:07.422
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:09.312
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.312 --> 00:00:12.242
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:13.982
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:13.982 --> 00:00:16.812
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.822 --> 00:00:18.822
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.822 --> 00:00:20.592
time as improving your English.

00:00:20.992 --> 00:00:24.382
I’m Alastair Budge, and today we are

00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:27.092
going to start a three-part mini-series

00:00:27.312 --> 00:00:30.032
called Tales from the Wild West.

00:00:30.532 --> 00:00:32.822
Instead of talking about the history

00:00:33.112 --> 00:00:34.952
of the Wild West, we’re going to

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:37.162
learn about it through the stories of

00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:39.662
three famous historical characters.

00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:42.792
In this episode, part one, it’ll be

00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:46.012
Wyatt Earp: a man who, to quote his

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.432
Wikipedia page, was at various points

00:00:48.482 --> 00:00:51.582
a Lawman, a buffalo hunter, a saloon

00:00:51.582 --> 00:00:54.852
keeper, a miner, a brothel keeper, and

00:00:54.852 --> 00:00:57.602
a boxing referee, which is a kind of

00:00:57.602 --> 00:00:59.902
resume you just don’t see nowadays.

00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:03.622
Next up it’ll be Bass Reeves: a man

00:01:03.632 --> 00:01:06.582
born into slavery who went on to

00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:08.602
become one of the most feared and

00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:11.302
successful lawmen in American history.

00:01:11.672 --> 00:01:13.672
And in part three, it’ll be

00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:16.252
Geronimo: the Native American leader

00:01:16.512 --> 00:01:18.602
whose life and surrender tell a

00:01:18.652 --> 00:01:20.582
very different story about the

00:01:20.602 --> 00:01:23.262
reality of life in the Wild West.

00:01:23.922 --> 00:01:26.172
OK then, let’s not waste a

00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:28.432
minute, and get right into it.

00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:33.056
It is the 26th of October, 1881.

00:01:33.502 --> 00:01:35.832
The time is around half

00:01:35.872 --> 00:01:37.922
past two in the afternoon.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:41.602
In a narrow vacant lot beside a

00:01:41.632 --> 00:01:45.082
photography studio in Tombstone, Arizona,

00:01:45.692 --> 00:01:48.332
eight men are standing close together.

00:01:48.832 --> 00:01:50.692
Close enough to touch.

00:01:51.492 --> 00:01:53.882
Some are pointing their guns.

00:01:54.212 --> 00:01:57.032
Others have not yet drawn their weapons.

00:01:57.802 --> 00:02:00.240
Thirty seconds later, it is over.

00:02:01.182 --> 00:02:03.562
One man has disappeared into

00:02:03.562 --> 00:02:05.372
a nearby photography studio.

00:02:06.062 --> 00:02:08.802
Three men are dead on the ground.

00:02:09.182 --> 00:02:10.452
Three others are wounded.

00:02:11.062 --> 00:02:13.622
One man, Wyatt Earp, is

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:15.952
standing without a scratch.

00:02:17.182 --> 00:02:20.292
This is the story of the gunfight at

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:22.652
the OK Corral, which has gone down

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:25.312
as perhaps the most iconic scene

00:02:25.592 --> 00:02:28.012
in the history of the Wild West.

00:02:29.252 --> 00:02:30.712
If you have seen one of the

00:02:30.722 --> 00:02:33.292
film adaptations of it, like

00:02:33.532 --> 00:02:35.402
Tombstone or the Wyatt Earp

00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:37.962
film, the scene will be familiar.

00:02:38.482 --> 00:02:41.492
But even if you haven’t, you can

00:02:41.532 --> 00:02:43.840
probably picture something like it.

00:02:44.422 --> 00:02:46.482
A long dusty street.

00:02:46.942 --> 00:02:49.802
Two groups of men walking slowly toward

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.902
each other in the afternoon heat.

00:02:52.532 --> 00:02:54.572
Flies buzzing around.

00:02:54.882 --> 00:02:57.062
The clink of spurs.

00:02:57.502 --> 00:03:01.132
A sheriff’s badge glinting in the sun.

00:03:02.132 --> 00:03:04.252
This is the Hollywood version.

00:03:05.102 --> 00:03:07.012
The real version was somewhat

00:03:07.052 --> 00:03:08.922
different, as we’ll come to discover.

00:03:09.992 --> 00:03:12.932
Now, before we get to the story of

00:03:12.952 --> 00:03:16.192
Wyatt Earp, we need some background.

00:03:17.222 --> 00:03:19.422
Let’s start with the phrase.

00:03:19.872 --> 00:03:24.301
The phrase “Wild West” probably

00:03:24.301 --> 00:03:25.332
conjures up a very specific image.

00:03:25.802 --> 00:03:26.352
Cattle.

00:03:26.722 --> 00:03:27.592
Saloons.

00:03:27.872 --> 00:03:29.472
Outlaws and sheriffs.

00:03:30.012 --> 00:03:31.592
Everyone looking like they

00:03:31.592 --> 00:03:33.822
wouldn’t say no to a hot bath.

00:03:34.732 --> 00:03:37.732
The Wild West, as historians generally

00:03:37.742 --> 00:03:41.442
use the term, refers to a specific period

00:03:41.592 --> 00:03:44.492
in American history: roughly from the

00:03:44.532 --> 00:03:48.352
end of the Civil War in 1865 to the

00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:51.362
early 1890s, although some people have

00:03:51.362 --> 00:03:53.482
it starting earlier and finishing later.

00:03:54.612 --> 00:03:57.452
This was the period when the United States

00:03:57.542 --> 00:04:00.932
was expanding westward at a ferocious

00:04:00.962 --> 00:04:04.072
pace, pushing into territories that had

00:04:04.072 --> 00:04:06.172
been home to Native American peoples

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.862
for centuries, and absorbing land that

00:04:09.872 --> 00:04:12.932
had until recently belonged to Mexico.

00:04:14.242 --> 00:04:17.482
What made it “wild”, from the perspective

00:04:17.482 --> 00:04:19.872
of the Americans arriving there, was

00:04:19.872 --> 00:04:22.892
the absence of established institutions.

00:04:23.192 --> 00:04:24.822
There were no courts.

00:04:24.832 --> 00:04:26.972
No reliable police forces.

00:04:27.072 --> 00:04:28.222
No prisons.

00:04:28.222 --> 00:04:29.812
No property registries.

00:04:30.302 --> 00:04:30.762
Nothing.

00:04:31.102 --> 00:04:33.722
It was, essentially, a free-for-all.

00:04:34.552 --> 00:04:37.992
Towns appeared almost overnight, and

00:04:38.142 --> 00:04:41.002
were typically built near to places where

00:04:41.202 --> 00:04:43.322
silver had been discovered [called silver

00:04:43.342 --> 00:04:46.112
strikes], or along cattle routes, or

00:04:46.272 --> 00:04:48.862
where new railway track had been laid.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:52.702
And the men who came to these places

00:04:53.112 --> 00:04:56.182
tended to be labourers, gamblers,

00:04:56.392 --> 00:04:59.402
former soldiers, and what we might call

00:04:59.652 --> 00:05:02.802
“drifters”: men who moved from place

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:04.862
to place without settling anywhere.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.302
Wyatt Earp was one of them.

00:05:09.302 --> 00:05:12.462
He was born in 1848, in

00:05:12.472 --> 00:05:14.472
Monmouth, Illinois, a small

00:05:14.472 --> 00:05:16.422
town in the American Midwest.

00:05:17.232 --> 00:05:20.022
He was the fourth of five brothers,

00:05:20.332 --> 00:05:22.962
and the family moved often during

00:05:22.972 --> 00:05:26.052
his childhood: Illinois to Iowa to

00:05:26.062 --> 00:05:28.862
Missouri to California and back again.

00:05:30.122 --> 00:05:32.742
History remembers him as a legendary

00:05:32.742 --> 00:05:35.932
lawman, a man who stood up for good

00:05:36.182 --> 00:05:39.112
in the face of lawlessness, but the

00:05:39.112 --> 00:05:41.272
reality is a little more complicated.

00:05:42.392 --> 00:05:45.462
In his twenties, he worked as a buffalo

00:05:45.462 --> 00:05:49.032
hunter on the Kansas plains, when the vast

00:05:49.032 --> 00:05:51.312
herds that had roamed the great plains

00:05:51.312 --> 00:05:53.752
for centuries were being systematically

00:05:53.782 --> 00:05:55.942
slaughtered for their hides.

00:05:56.592 --> 00:05:59.852
Then, he drove stagecoaches: horse-drawn

00:05:59.852 --> 00:06:01.922
vehicles that carried passengers and

00:06:01.932 --> 00:06:04.742
mail across long distances before

00:06:04.742 --> 00:06:06.112
the railways reached everywhere.

00:06:06.982 --> 00:06:09.272
He was a taxi driver, essentially.

00:06:10.662 --> 00:06:14.132
In 1871, he was arrested on a charge

00:06:14.152 --> 00:06:16.702
of horse theft, which was a serious

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:18.882
crime at the time, and for which

00:06:18.912 --> 00:06:21.772
execution wasn’t an uncommon sentence.

00:06:22.992 --> 00:06:24.922
Luckily for him, and for our

00:06:24.932 --> 00:06:26.772
story, he managed to escape

00:06:26.802 --> 00:06:29.082
from jail, and skipped town.

00:06:30.242 --> 00:06:32.362
And again, fortunately for our

00:06:32.372 --> 00:06:35.912
story, the 1870s was not a time

00:06:35.912 --> 00:06:37.982
of extensive background checks and

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.482
synchronised criminal databases, so

00:06:40.482 --> 00:06:43.132
Earp was able to get various jobs as

00:06:43.132 --> 00:06:45.482
a lawman, a sort of police officer.

00:06:46.482 --> 00:06:48.582
First in Wichita and then in

00:06:48.592 --> 00:06:51.442
Dodge City, both in Kansas, where

00:06:51.442 --> 00:06:53.592
he served as a deputy marshal.

00:06:54.432 --> 00:06:57.012
So, he has had what we might call a

00:06:57.362 --> 00:07:00.662
“portfolio career” so far: buffalo

00:07:00.662 --> 00:07:03.212
hunter, stagecoach driver, horse

00:07:03.212 --> 00:07:05.562
thief, and now deputy marshal.

00:07:06.632 --> 00:07:09.392
He was also, it would appear, involved

00:07:09.402 --> 00:07:12.102
in prostitution, and was arrested

00:07:12.112 --> 00:07:14.972
several times for running a brothel.

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:18.482
And, to complete the picture, he

00:07:18.482 --> 00:07:20.942
was a frequent gambler, spending

00:07:20.952 --> 00:07:23.531
much of his time in saloons and

00:07:23.531 --> 00:07:25.762
playing a card game called faro.

00:07:26.472 --> 00:07:28.242
He was actually quite good, he

00:07:28.242 --> 00:07:30.532
would later claim, and made more

00:07:30.532 --> 00:07:32.552
money through gambling than he did

00:07:32.562 --> 00:07:34.941
through his official job as a lawman.

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:40.032
By 1879, he had made his way to a

00:07:40.072 --> 00:07:43.162
town called Tombstone, in Arizona,

00:07:43.492 --> 00:07:46.112
along with three of his five brothers.

00:07:47.129 --> 00:07:50.252
One brother, Virgil, was already there,

00:07:50.562 --> 00:07:53.392
and had taken up the position of town

00:07:53.392 --> 00:07:56.812
marshal, the local law enforcement chief.

00:07:58.092 --> 00:08:01.082
Virgil had not just arrived first; he

00:08:01.082 --> 00:08:03.062
had effectively sent for his brothers.

00:08:03.802 --> 00:08:06.422
As town marshal, he could appoint

00:08:06.422 --> 00:08:09.372
deputies, and he hired family.

00:08:10.032 --> 00:08:13.182
It was nepotism, essentially, but it

00:08:13.182 --> 00:08:16.882
was also practical: in a town with

00:08:16.882 --> 00:08:19.992
no reliable law enforcement, men

00:08:20.312 --> 00:08:22.482
who would take a bullet for you were

00:08:22.482 --> 00:08:24.642
worth more than qualified strangers.

00:08:25.652 --> 00:08:28.292
James, the oldest of the four, who

00:08:28.292 --> 00:08:31.072
was quieter and more reserved than

00:08:31.151 --> 00:08:33.392
the others, he worked in a saloon.

00:08:34.122 --> 00:08:36.422
Another, Morgan, arrived

00:08:36.452 --> 00:08:38.042
around the same time as Wyatt.

00:08:38.532 --> 00:08:41.382
And Warren, the youngest, came later.

00:08:42.772 --> 00:08:46.906
Tombstone had struck silver in 1877,

00:08:47.322 --> 00:08:50.842
and by 1881 it was one of the most

00:08:50.972 --> 00:08:53.502
prosperous cities in the area, with a

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:55.652
population of several thousand people.

00:08:56.259 --> 00:08:57.982
It had newspapers.

00:08:58.082 --> 00:08:59.042
An opera house.

00:08:59.362 --> 00:09:00.482
A telegraph office.

00:09:00.782 --> 00:09:03.122
It was pretty big by frontier standards.

00:09:03.952 --> 00:09:06.182
It also had a serious problem.

00:09:06.752 --> 00:09:09.322
The problem was the Cowboys.

00:09:10.062 --> 00:09:11.792
The Cowboys, which is what they

00:09:11.792 --> 00:09:14.042
were actually called, they were

00:09:14.052 --> 00:09:16.952
a loose network of cattle thieves

00:09:16.952 --> 00:09:19.442
and smugglers who operated out of

00:09:19.452 --> 00:09:21.212
the surrounding hills and valleys.

00:09:21.702 --> 00:09:25.032
They stole livestock in Mexico and sold

00:09:25.032 --> 00:09:27.152
it on the American side of the border.

00:09:27.412 --> 00:09:29.362
They robbed stagecoaches.

00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:31.132
They intimidated people.

00:09:31.822 --> 00:09:34.222
They were not organised in any

00:09:34.442 --> 00:09:37.282
formal sense, but they had numbers,

00:09:37.332 --> 00:09:39.402
and local political support.

00:09:40.616 --> 00:09:43.716
And as you might imagine, they hated

00:09:43.906 --> 00:09:46.566
the Earp brothers, and had a running

00:09:46.566 --> 00:09:49.186
feud that had been building for months.

00:09:49.866 --> 00:09:52.326
The tension was about money and

00:09:52.406 --> 00:09:54.696
power and political allegiance.

00:09:55.276 --> 00:09:58.146
The county sheriff, a man named Johnny

00:09:58.146 --> 00:10:01.046
Behan, was sympathetic to the Cowboys.

00:10:01.566 --> 00:10:04.356
He had grown up among ranching people

00:10:04.456 --> 00:10:06.806
and shared their wariness of the Earp

00:10:06.806 --> 00:10:10.276
brothers, who he saw as outsiders with

00:10:10.276 --> 00:10:13.056
their own agenda, which they were, really.

00:10:13.926 --> 00:10:17.286
Virgil Earp, as Tombstone’s town marshal,

00:10:17.556 --> 00:10:20.676
was not sympathetic to the Cowboys at all.

00:10:21.166 --> 00:10:23.786
And Wyatt Earp had ambitions to

00:10:23.786 --> 00:10:25.596
become county sheriff himself.

00:10:26.046 --> 00:10:27.186
In other words, he had

00:10:27.186 --> 00:10:29.136
his sights on Behan’s job.

00:10:30.226 --> 00:10:32.676
There was also a personal dimension.

00:10:33.386 --> 00:10:36.056
Johnny Behan and Wyatt Earp had

00:10:36.056 --> 00:10:38.056
been involved with the same woman,

00:10:38.382 --> 00:10:41.282
Josephine Marcus, a theatre performer

00:10:41.642 --> 00:10:43.462
who had been briefly engaged to

00:10:43.462 --> 00:10:45.322
Behan before she broke it off.

00:10:46.862 --> 00:10:50.482
By the autumn of 1881, the feud

00:10:50.632 --> 00:10:52.972
between the Earps and the Cowboys

00:10:53.092 --> 00:10:55.402
had been simmering for long enough.

00:10:56.402 --> 00:11:00.452
On the morning of October 26th, Virgil

00:11:00.452 --> 00:11:03.592
Earp received word that several Cowboys

00:11:03.592 --> 00:11:06.612
were in town and visibly armed, which

00:11:07.032 --> 00:11:09.572
technically violated Tombstone’s

00:11:09.612 --> 00:11:12.462
ordinance, its local law, against

00:11:12.532 --> 00:11:15.152
carrying firearms within the city limits.

00:11:16.282 --> 00:11:20.122
Virgil assembled a small group: Wyatt,

00:11:20.712 --> 00:11:23.472
Morgan, and their friend Doc Holliday,

00:11:23.682 --> 00:11:25.912
a gambler and gunfighter who was one

00:11:25.912 --> 00:11:28.142
of the most feared men on the frontier.

00:11:29.472 --> 00:11:33.112
The four of them walked toward the vacant

00:11:33.132 --> 00:11:35.592
lot where they had heard the Cowboys were.

00:11:36.712 --> 00:11:39.002
What happened next is disputed

00:11:39.422 --> 00:11:40.882
in almost every account.

00:11:41.782 --> 00:11:43.312
Who fired first?

00:11:43.632 --> 00:11:45.282
Was there a verbal warning?

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.552
Were the Cowboys trying to surrender?

00:11:48.122 --> 00:11:49.942
Were the Cowboys even armed?

00:11:50.792 --> 00:11:53.442
Virgil Earp later said he told

00:11:53.442 --> 00:11:54.872
them to give up their weapons.

00:11:55.292 --> 00:11:56.932
The Cowboys’ side said

00:11:56.982 --> 00:11:59.191
no such offer was made.

00:12:00.451 --> 00:12:02.862
What everyone agrees on is this:

00:12:03.432 --> 00:12:05.702
it lasted roughly thirty seconds.

00:12:06.282 --> 00:12:08.462
On the Cowboys side, three

00:12:08.572 --> 00:12:10.029
were killed outright.

00:12:10.632 --> 00:12:12.642
On the other side, Virgil and

00:12:12.652 --> 00:12:14.612
Morgan Earp were both wounded.

00:12:15.062 --> 00:12:16.882
Doc Holliday was grazed.

00:12:17.442 --> 00:12:20.172
Wyatt walked away without injury.

00:12:21.352 --> 00:12:23.012
There were no celebrations

00:12:23.172 --> 00:12:24.712
among the town’s residents.

00:12:25.042 --> 00:12:26.842
The Earps and Holliday

00:12:27.032 --> 00:12:28.792
were charged with murder.

00:12:29.872 --> 00:12:32.532
There was a trial, which ultimately

00:12:32.692 --> 00:12:34.842
ruled in their favour, finding

00:12:35.092 --> 00:12:37.072
that Virgil had been acting in

00:12:37.072 --> 00:12:39.432
his capacity as town marshal and

00:12:39.432 --> 00:12:41.592
that the shootings were justified.

00:12:42.362 --> 00:12:44.902
But the community was divided.

00:12:45.592 --> 00:12:47.672
Half of Tombstone saw the

00:12:47.702 --> 00:12:49.872
Earps as law enforcers.

00:12:50.252 --> 00:12:51.972
The other half saw them as

00:12:51.982 --> 00:12:54.362
men who had used their badges

00:12:54.642 --> 00:12:56.482
to settle a private score.

00:12:57.752 --> 00:12:58.652
There were elements of

00:12:58.652 --> 00:13:00.652
truth to both arguments.

00:13:01.062 --> 00:13:03.922
Virgil Earp was the town marshal, and

00:13:03.932 --> 00:13:06.312
the Cowboys were breaking the rules

00:13:06.452 --> 00:13:08.542
about carrying weapons inside the town.

00:13:09.322 --> 00:13:11.961
But the Earps had spent months

00:13:12.432 --> 00:13:13.902
feuding with the Cowboys.

00:13:14.192 --> 00:13:15.862
And the badge gave them the

00:13:15.892 --> 00:13:18.239
authority to do something about it.

00:13:19.662 --> 00:13:22.722
And this shootout was just the start

00:13:22.912 --> 00:13:26.022
of a bitter tit-for-tat vendetta.

00:13:27.172 --> 00:13:29.132
Two months later, in December

00:13:29.372 --> 00:13:33.002
1881, Virgil Earp was ambushed

00:13:33.162 --> 00:13:35.302
outside a saloon late at night.

00:13:36.072 --> 00:13:38.403
Shotgun blasts hit his left arm.

00:13:39.211 --> 00:13:41.062
He survived, but his arm

00:13:41.062 --> 00:13:42.532
was permanently disabled.

00:13:43.452 --> 00:13:46.921
Three months after that, in March 1882,

00:13:47.352 --> 00:13:50.142
Morgan Earp was shot through a window

00:13:50.382 --> 00:13:52.532
while playing billiards in a saloon.

00:13:53.632 --> 00:13:54.742
Two bullets hit him.

00:13:55.262 --> 00:13:57.722
He died within the hour, on the

00:13:57.722 --> 00:14:00.362
floor, in his brother Wyatt’s arms.

00:14:01.552 --> 00:14:04.732
And what followed was not due legal

00:14:04.752 --> 00:14:08.672
process, it was revenge, pure and simple.

00:14:09.342 --> 00:14:11.892
Wyatt Earp, with a small group of men

00:14:11.982 --> 00:14:14.032
that included Doc Holliday, Warren

00:14:14.032 --> 00:14:17.062
Earp, and several others, rode out in

00:14:17.062 --> 00:14:20.072
pursuit of the men they held responsible.

00:14:21.402 --> 00:14:23.572
Over the following three weeks,

00:14:23.762 --> 00:14:26.312
they hunted and killed at least four

00:14:26.312 --> 00:14:28.742
of them; at a railway station, in

00:14:28.742 --> 00:14:31.592
the hills, and at a water source.

00:14:32.612 --> 00:14:34.532
These were not arrests.

00:14:34.572 --> 00:14:36.632
There was no due process.

00:14:36.632 --> 00:14:37.532
No trial.

00:14:38.352 --> 00:14:40.482
This was the “Wild” West, but

00:14:40.872 --> 00:14:43.542
even here, this crossed a line.

00:14:44.802 --> 00:14:46.722
A warrant was issued for Wyatt

00:14:46.722 --> 00:14:49.732
Earp’s arrest on charges of murder.

00:14:50.422 --> 00:14:53.352
He fled Arizona Territory, rode north into

00:14:53.352 --> 00:14:56.562
Colorado and never returned to Arizona.

00:14:57.718 --> 00:14:59.810
He bounced from place to place,

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:01.810
chasing the next opportunity.

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:04.170
Colorado, then Texas.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:08.050
San Diego in the late 1880s, where he ran

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.400
saloons and became a property speculator.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:13.790
He made good money, then lost most

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:15.960
of it when the land boom collapsed.

00:15:16.693 --> 00:15:19.839
In 1896, he made the news after

00:15:19.839 --> 00:15:21.839
refereeing a heavyweight boxing

00:15:21.839 --> 00:15:24.029
championship fight in San Francisco.

00:15:24.669 --> 00:15:26.779
The bout ended controversially,

00:15:26.959 --> 00:15:28.899
with Earp awarding the match to

00:15:28.899 --> 00:15:30.599
the fighter who appeared to be

00:15:30.609 --> 00:15:33.229
losing, and the national newspapers

00:15:33.379 --> 00:15:35.539
accused him of fixing the result.

00:15:36.269 --> 00:15:39.029
Then came Alaska during the gold rush

00:15:39.029 --> 00:15:42.599
of 1897, where he ran a saloon and

00:15:42.599 --> 00:15:44.539
reportedly made the equivalent of

00:15:44.539 --> 00:15:46.959
several million dollars in today’s money.

00:15:47.459 --> 00:15:50.069
Then it was Nevada, still gambling.

00:15:50.599 --> 00:15:52.409
And finally Los Angeles.

00:15:53.289 --> 00:15:55.989
And it would be in Los Angeles that

00:15:55.989 --> 00:15:59.029
he would find himself remade, turned

00:15:59.369 --> 00:16:01.399
into something that he never was.

00:16:02.419 --> 00:16:04.529
Los Angeles, of course, is the

00:16:04.529 --> 00:16:07.109
home of Hollywood, which was, by

00:16:07.109 --> 00:16:10.739
the 1910s and 1920s, the centre

00:16:11.019 --> 00:16:12.799
of the American film industry.

00:16:13.869 --> 00:16:16.289
The dominant genre was the Western.

00:16:16.759 --> 00:16:19.779
Cowboys and sheriffs and open plains.

00:16:20.169 --> 00:16:21.479
Clear moral lines.

00:16:21.839 --> 00:16:23.559
The good man with the gun.

00:16:24.479 --> 00:16:27.249
Now, Earp had been thinking about fame

00:16:27.249 --> 00:16:29.569
and how it worked for a long time.

00:16:30.229 --> 00:16:32.729
A generation earlier, he’d seen

00:16:32.729 --> 00:16:35.049
how Wild West figures had become

00:16:35.059 --> 00:16:37.459
household names through dime

00:16:37.459 --> 00:16:40.129
novels, the cheap, sensational

00:16:40.139 --> 00:16:42.429
books sold for about ten cents.

00:16:43.059 --> 00:16:45.569
A man who got written up in the right

00:16:45.589 --> 00:16:48.289
way could become famous across the

00:16:48.289 --> 00:16:51.059
country, have his name outlast him.

00:16:51.941 --> 00:16:54.199
Earp had watched it happen to others

00:16:54.509 --> 00:16:56.419
and wanted it to happen to him.

00:16:57.309 --> 00:17:01.018
And now Hollywood was doing the same

00:17:01.019 --> 00:17:04.487
thing, albeit on a far larger scale.

00:17:05.497 --> 00:17:08.369
And importantly, Earp had something

00:17:08.389 --> 00:17:10.649
that almost nobody else on those

00:17:10.659 --> 00:17:14.369
film sets had: real-life experience.

00:17:14.559 --> 00:17:15.879
He was actually there.

00:17:16.379 --> 00:17:18.109
They were making movies about

00:17:18.149 --> 00:17:20.279
a period he had lived through.

00:17:21.669 --> 00:17:24.379
He became a regular visitor to Western

00:17:24.379 --> 00:17:26.859
sets, and got to know some of the

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:29.529
biggest stars of the era, not just

00:17:29.529 --> 00:17:32.229
because he enjoyed it, but because he

00:17:32.279 --> 00:17:34.709
understood what Hollywood could do.

00:17:35.549 --> 00:17:37.839
These were the people who would

00:17:37.839 --> 00:17:40.519
decide whose story got told.

00:17:41.839 --> 00:17:43.569
He advised them on how to

00:17:43.569 --> 00:17:45.759
carry themselves, how to hold a

00:17:45.759 --> 00:17:48.804
pistol, how a real lawman moved.

00:17:49.399 --> 00:17:51.529
And all the while, he was

00:17:51.529 --> 00:17:53.949
making a case for himself as a

00:17:53.999 --> 00:17:57.099
quintessential hero of the Wild West.

00:17:58.129 --> 00:18:00.009
But he had a problem.

00:18:00.569 --> 00:18:03.129
The record as it existed was

00:18:03.129 --> 00:18:05.369
not the one he wanted told.

00:18:06.169 --> 00:18:08.439
In the newspapers of the Southwest,

00:18:08.669 --> 00:18:11.318
he had been reported as a murderer: a

00:18:11.398 --> 00:18:14.579
man who had used his badge to pursue a

00:18:14.599 --> 00:18:17.729
private vendetta and then fled justice.

00:18:18.269 --> 00:18:20.209
The boxing scandal had brought

00:18:20.209 --> 00:18:22.209
his name to a national audience,

00:18:22.539 --> 00:18:24.639
but for the wrong reasons.

00:18:25.489 --> 00:18:28.509
In other words, the name Wyatt Earp was

00:18:28.509 --> 00:18:31.519
associated with crime, match-fixing,

00:18:31.889 --> 00:18:35.029
and general skulduggery, dodgy dealing;

00:18:35.849 --> 00:18:39.009
he wanted to be remembered as a hero.

00:18:39.929 --> 00:18:42.519
So, he got to work trying to

00:18:42.609 --> 00:18:44.669
change the historical record.

00:18:45.489 --> 00:18:47.829
He tried first with a writer named

00:18:47.899 --> 00:18:50.669
John Flood, who spent years working

00:18:50.689 --> 00:18:53.019
on a manuscript about Earp’s life.

00:18:54.069 --> 00:18:56.509
Flood was reportedly a terrible

00:18:56.539 --> 00:18:59.139
writer, and the book was rejected

00:18:59.139 --> 00:19:01.349
by every publisher it was sent to.

00:19:02.519 --> 00:19:06.039
Then, in the last years of his life, Earp

00:19:06.299 --> 00:19:09.369
found a journalist named Stuart Lake.

00:19:10.379 --> 00:19:13.309
Lake was ambitious, a gifted writer,

00:19:13.659 --> 00:19:16.029
and willing to sit with the old man

00:19:16.249 --> 00:19:18.886
and take down everything he said.

00:19:19.477 --> 00:19:20.421
Earp talked.

00:19:20.849 --> 00:19:22.959
Lake listened and took notes.

00:19:23.529 --> 00:19:25.989
Between them, they worked for the

00:19:25.999 --> 00:19:28.609
definitive account: the one that

00:19:28.609 --> 00:19:31.229
would fix his reputation for good.

00:19:32.596 --> 00:19:35.449
Earp died on the 13th of January,

00:19:35.649 --> 00:19:38.589
1929, before the book was finished.

00:19:39.289 --> 00:19:40.779
He was eighty years old.

00:19:41.769 --> 00:19:44.459
By this time, the gunfight at the

00:19:44.499 --> 00:19:47.259
OK Corral was almost fifty years

00:19:47.279 --> 00:19:49.499
in the past and barely remembered.

00:19:50.619 --> 00:19:54.209
But two years later, he and the

00:19:54.239 --> 00:19:56.969
gunfight would be brought back to life.

00:19:57.459 --> 00:19:59.299
Lake published his book:

00:19:59.769 --> 00:20:02.499
Wyatt Earp: Frontier Marshal.

00:20:03.189 --> 00:20:06.269
The Earp of this book, a book that was

00:20:06.289 --> 00:20:08.409
presented as an accurate biography,

00:20:08.809 --> 00:20:11.929
was not the gambling, drifting,

00:20:12.319 --> 00:20:15.009
horse-thieving, pimping, revenge-killing,

00:20:15.049 --> 00:20:17.689
complicated man of the historical record.

00:20:18.229 --> 00:20:19.899
He was a hero.

00:20:20.409 --> 00:20:21.139
A symbol.

00:20:21.509 --> 00:20:23.889
The embodiment of law and

00:20:23.969 --> 00:20:26.309
order in a lawless land.

00:20:27.339 --> 00:20:29.289
The book was a bestseller.

00:20:29.829 --> 00:20:31.139
It was adapted for film

00:20:31.459 --> 00:20:33.729
almost immediately, and the

00:20:33.759 --> 00:20:35.359
adaptations kept coming.

00:20:36.069 --> 00:20:39.589
John Ford’s My Darling Clementine in 1946.

00:20:39.899 --> 00:20:43.429
Gunfight at the O.K. Corral in 1957.

00:20:43.919 --> 00:20:47.829
And then, in 1993 and 1994, two

00:20:47.869 --> 00:20:50.439
major Hollywood films, Tombstone

00:20:50.649 --> 00:20:52.979
and Wyatt Earp, brought the

00:20:52.979 --> 00:20:55.309
story to yet another generation.

00:20:56.019 --> 00:20:58.999
With each retelling, the thirty-second

00:20:59.049 --> 00:21:01.619
argument over a town firearms

00:21:01.649 --> 00:21:04.239
ordinance became a defining battle

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:07.239
between civilisation and lawlessness.

00:21:07.869 --> 00:21:09.959
A drifting gambler with a chequered

00:21:09.999 --> 00:21:13.019
past became the man who tamed the West.

00:21:13.849 --> 00:21:17.489
So, to wrap things up, Wyatt Earp died

00:21:17.499 --> 00:21:20.719
in 1929 without ever clearing his name,

00:21:20.919 --> 00:21:22.829
without ever seeing his book published,

00:21:23.209 --> 00:21:25.959
without ever appearing on a cinema screen.

00:21:26.959 --> 00:21:28.949
Within a few years of his death,

00:21:29.169 --> 00:21:30.899
he was one of the most famous

00:21:30.899 --> 00:21:32.819
figures of the American West.

00:21:33.999 --> 00:21:36.409
The Wild West had found its hero.

00:21:37.039 --> 00:21:38.749
The only problem was that

00:21:38.759 --> 00:21:40.809
he’d never really existed.

00:21:42.949 --> 00:21:45.429
OK, then, that is it for today’s

00:21:45.429 --> 00:21:48.230
episode: the first part of our three-part

00:21:48.488 --> 00:21:51.549
mini-series on Tales from the Wild West.

00:21:51.989 --> 00:21:55.589
Next up, we’ll meet Bass Reeves: born into

00:21:55.589 --> 00:21:58.509
slavery, three thousand arrests to his

00:21:58.509 --> 00:22:01.109
name, but was almost forgotten to history.

00:22:01.439 --> 00:22:03.429
And in part three, we’ll look at

00:22:03.439 --> 00:22:05.539
the Wild West from an entirely

00:22:05.549 --> 00:22:07.739
different angle: through the story

00:22:07.929 --> 00:22:10.361
of the Apache leader Geronimo.

00:22:10.822 --> 00:22:12.662
You’ve been listening to English Learning

00:22:12.662 --> 00:22:15.262
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:22:15.592 --> 00:22:18.502
I’m Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:22:18.502 --> 00:22:20.692
I’ll catch you in the next episode.

